NAME
       theme - console colour scheme, palette and background

SYNOPSIS
       theme
       theme <name> [--once]
       theme bg #RRGGBB
       theme fg #RRGGBB
       theme -r

DESCRIPTION
       Sets the sixteen colours the console draws with, the default
       text colour and the background. With no arguments it prints the
       current scheme and lists the rest, drawing each one in its own
       colours so the listing shows what you would get.

       The choice is written to /etc/console.conf and restored at boot,
       next to the console font. --once applies a scheme without
       saving it, and -r returns to the original palette and forgets
       the file.

       bg and fg change one colour on its own, taking a hex value with
       or without the leading #.

       Changing the scheme clears the screen: cells keep the colours
       they were written with, so leaving the old ones in place would
       show half the screen in the previous palette.

MAKING YOUR OWN
       theme edit [name] opens a full-screen editor, starting from an
       existing scheme when one is named. Up and down choose which of
       the eighteen colours to work on - sixteen palette entries plus
       the default text and background - left and right choose the red,
       green or blue channel, and - and + adjust it, by sixteen with
       the shifted pair. h types a value as #RRGGBB, n sets the name
       and s saves.

       The console follows every change as it is made, so the editor's
       own colours are the preview.

       Themes are written to /etc/themes as plain text and appear in
       the listing beside the built-in ones, marked custom.

SCHEMES
       classic      the VGA palette the console started with
       cervus       soft slate, meant for long sessions
       gruv         warm, low contrast
       solar        muted blue-grey
       paper        dark text on a light page
       matrix       green on black and nothing else
       dracula      purple night
       catppuccin   mocha, warm and muted
       latte        the same in daylight
       nord         cold blue, low contrast
       rose         dusty pink and mauve
       sand, mint   pastel, one warm and one cool
       amber        an old amber terminal
       mono         grey scale

FILES
       /etc/console.conf   the chosen scheme, restored at boot
       /etc/themes/*.theme custom schemes

SEE ALSO
       setfont(1), mode(1), csh(1)
